now let's take a look at how we actually  conduct uh variant calling on some data  so first we will go to the tbionfor  platform where we will find in areas of  analysis section so in this areas of  analysis you can see there are many  different sections related to  transcriptomic genomic metagenomic and  other kinds of data here we will look  for mutation variance section if you  hover over the eye icon you will get  more information you can see that this  one is going to be appropriate for exam  sequencing data if we go within this  section just click on the title  we will be prompted to upload data this  data can come from ncbi  from our computer or from the ftp  account if you have a research account  with us but for now we will use the demo  option so we will click on this drop  down and here let's select this last  example for breast cancer mutation so  let's  click on this link and what you should  see is an interface for the pipeline  builder you will only get an option to  select something that is highlighted and  you can see that the first thing that is  highlighted is the start button  here you will have a little bit of  information about what this pipeline is  all about and it's going to be about  finding variance in this data now once  we've started we have our next option to  select and that is bow tie two in each  one of these steps you will get  information about the algorithm and  briefly what bow tie two does is it  hashes all of the sequences of the reads  that we have in our fastq file arranges  them so that it is quick to find the  most appropriate position on the  reference genome based on the matching  sequence  now you can also click on input files  and you can see what we have to upload  to be able to use this section and this  includes a fastq file for each one of  the samples that we have and we have  several samples here it will also have a  link to the reference genome this is  important because you can see that each  one of the files that we originally  uploaded is an sra format s array is a  format to compress raw fastview files  because they are so large  after we've mapped our reads onto the  reference genome what we get is a bam  file or a sam file that contains the  information about each read and the  position on the genome where this read  aligns to we can visualize this data so  let's click on visualization  and here you can look at input files and  you can see that we have the fast q  files but also these sam files and like  i said  fast you files are reads  just their sequences in random order and  the sam file is when they are organized  also by position on the reference genome  so after we have visualized the data we  also want to determine whether any  variance can be called on the collection  of reeds that fall in a specific region  and for that we will use free base  freebase is a haplotype-based variant  detector and it's a tool that works  really fast it takes in the bam files  which is already the reads that have  been aligned to the reference genome it  looks at how many reads contain a  specific change from the reference to be  able to call that a variant  now the output file if you click here is  going to be a vcf and that is the vcf  that we will later work with to  determine whether any of the variants  are actually clinically relevant  now let's click on end  okay after your page reloads you will  click here on the visualization button  the visualization opens up in jbrowse  and jbrowse is a software that allows  you to visualize the same file the index  genome  and actually look through the different  output files that we have in the  pipeline so here down below you have  what's called mapping statistics it  tells you how many of the reads were  paired how many aligned correctly and  how many aligned incorrectly and also  gives you an overall alignment rate so  you can see that for each sample  we have an alignment rate and it's very  good it's over 97  so that means that the alignment worked  really well  one of the samples is a little bit below  that 96.76  but that is still pretty good now here  on the side you will have multiple  tracks you can open these tracks let's  just take one sample and then you can  also look at the vcf file for variant  calling which means that it will only  show us whether a certain position is a  variant as you scroll through  you will find that some segments have no  reads at all and others will have some  read so here each bar like this  represents a read so let's try searching  for a gene one important gene  in cancer is p53 p53 is the name of the  protein and the name of the gene is tp  53  as you type it in here you can then  select the gene and now you will see  that this particular gene has a lot of  reads aligned to the reference so over  hovering over each position you will  have the count of total reads that are  actually mapped to this region  down here you will have your  gene  in the gtf file so we'll display all of  the transcripts that are available for  this gene the thick bars are exons and  the lines are introns or non-coding  regions  you can see that approximately the  accumulation of reeds corresponds  to the exons that we have even though it  is not precise  in the middle you can see the  information about each one of the  variants and you can see that there are  different kinds  single nucleotide variant  substitution  insertion  here you will have an information  about this specific  variant that is identified for example  for this insertion you can see  that  t-a-c-a-a  turned into t-a-c-a-a-c-a-a  so we have an additional three  nucleotides that were inserted in this  position
 

